        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01KiibH8vc6dPx3gwVQXHHnT",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/circuit-breaker.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Circuit Breaker Pattern\\ndescription: A state machine pattern that prevents cascading failure by wrapping unreliable dependencies with closed, open, and half-open states.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - pattern/CircuitBreaker\\n  - concept/Resilience\\n  - concept/Fault-Tolerance\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single authoritative source providing clear definition of pattern mechanics; half-open state behavior confirmed but not elaborated beyond trial-call decision mechanism.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nThe circuit breaker pattern protects against cascading failure by wrapping calls to an unreliable or struggling downstream dependency with a state machine. It allows the dependency room to recover rather than overwhelming it with repeated failed requests.\\n\\n## State Machine\\n\\nThe circuit breaker operates in three states:\\n\\n- **Closed**: Calls pass through to the dependency normally. The circuit is in healthy operation.\\n- **Open**: Calls fail fast without attempting to reach the dependency. No traffic flows through, giving the downstream service a chance to recover.\\n- **Half-Open**: A trial call is executed to determine whether the dependency has recovered. The outcome of this trial call decides whether the circuit closes again (calls resume) or opens again (calls continue to fail fast).\\n\\n## Half-Open State: Trial-Call Behavior\\n\\nThe half-open state is the circuit breaker\\u0027s recovery mechanism. When the circuit transitions from open to half-open (typically after a timeout), a single trial call is sent to the dependency:\\n\\n- If the trial call **succeeds**, the circuit closes and normal operation resumes.\\n- If the trial call **fails**, the circuit opens again, returning to fast-fail mode and resetting any recovery timeout.\\n\\nThis trial-call approach allows the circuit breaker to detect when a struggling service has recovered without subjecting it to full traffic load immediately upon any sign of improvement.\\n\\n## Purpose\\n\\nBy preventing cascading failure, the circuit breaker gives a struggling downstream service the breathing room it needs to recover rather than being buried under continued requests. This improves overall system resilience.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: eval://steering-adoption/sample-06\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
